          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Economic and Legal Reforms</strong><br>\nCromwell's administration focused on making legal and economic reforms to improve the efficiency of government and the welfare of his subjects. He implemented new administrative practices and supported trade and commerce, which helped England's economy during his tenure.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Foreign Policy Under Cromwell</strong><br>\nCromwell's foreign policy was assertive and expanded English influence abroad. He waged successful military campaigns against the Dutch and the Spanish, which improved England's standing in Europe and facilitated the growth of the English empire through territorial acquisitions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy of Cromwell</strong><br>\nOliver Cromwell died on September 3, 1658, and was succeeded by his son Richard. His legacy is controversial; some view him as a defender of liberty against tyranny, while others see him as a dictator. After the restoration of the monarchy in 1660, his body was exhumed and subjected to a posthumous execution, reflecting the deep divisions he inspired in English society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
